{#
    `.. configuration-block::` — the same setting written in two languages.

    The core gives this node a tab markup of its own: a `role="tablist"` of
    buttons, a panel each, and `display: none` on all but the first. The
    switching is a script the renderer does not ship, so left alone it is a row
    of buttons that do nothing above a block that cannot be reached.

    It is the same content as `.. tabs::` and it becomes the same element, for
    the reason that decides every other template here: a reader must not have
    to learn which directive an author reached for. What is dropped is the
    tablist markup — `sds-tabs` builds its own, with the arrow keys — and the
    panel ids, which only that script used.

    **The labels are a property**, for the reason `body/directive/tabs` states:
    an element rendered before the browser has no items to read them off, so a
    set that says them only on its panels arrives with an empty bar.

    `sync` is what this directive is for and `.. tabs::` is not: the same
    setting appears on a page four times, and the language is chosen once. The
    word is fixed here rather than offered to an author — two blocks of the
    same document that did not agree would be the bug this prevents.
#}
{%- set labels = [] -%}
{%- for tab in node.tabs -%}
    {%- set labels = labels|merge([tab.label]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}
<sds-tabs sync="configuration" items="{{ labels|json_encode }}">
    {%- for tab in node.tabs %}
        <sds-tab-item label="{{ tab.label }}">
            {{- renderNode(tab.content) -}}
        </sds-tab-item>
    {%- endfor %}
</sds-tabs>
